.TH SYSTEM 3
.SH NAME
system \- issue a shell command 
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <stdlib.h>
.PP
.B int system(const char *string);
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I System
causes the
.I string
to be given to
.IR  sh (1)
as input as if the string had been typed as a command
at a terminal.
The current process waits until the shell has
completed, then returns the exit status of the shell.
.PP
.B Note:
a null
.I string
asks whether a command processor exists, which is C11 sec 7.22.4.8.  v7 had no
such case.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
popen(3),
exec(2), wait(2)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Exit status 127 indicates the shell couldn't be executed.
.SH FILES
share/besm6/lib/libc.a
.br
lib/libc/stdio/system.c
.SH BESM-6 NOTES
The status is
.IR wait (2)'s
and is
.B only 15 bits wide here
\- it comes back in an index register \- so exit codes 128..255 arrive truncated.
See
.IR wait (2).
.PP
.B This routine is tested in both harnesses and they test opposite things.
Under b6sim, /bin/sh is a host binary and not a BESM-6
.IR a.out ,
so the child always fails to exec and reports it with the 127 above; that is
lib/test/spawn, and it is a b6sim-only test for exactly that reason.  Off the
disk image, /bin/sh really does start, and lib/test/shellt is the other half.
